Implementation notes — Pong replacement

WHY the design:
- Single RAF loop in Pong.ts (mirrors the prior SpaceInvaders pattern) so update
  order is deterministic and simulation stops cleanly on win/lose. update(dt) is
  public and driven directly by unit tests; the loop only advances in `playing`.
- dt clamp (0.05s) prevents ball/paddle teleporting after a backgrounded tab —
  addresses the spec's large-dt edge case together with per-entity clamping.
- Reflection angle derived from (ballCenterY - paddleCenterY)/(halfHeight),
  clamped to ±BALL_MAX_BOUNCE_ANGLE (60°, < 90° so the ball always makes
  horizontal progress). Centre hit ≈ horizontal, edge hit ≈ steep — prevents the
  "always straight back" degenerate rally the spec calls out.
- Collision only fires when the ball moves *into* a paddle (vx sign) and the ball
  is repositioned to the paddle face on contact, so it can't re-trigger while
  overlapping on the way out (no double-bounce sticking, no tunneling).
- Scoring recentres the ball and arms SERVE_DELAY immediately, so one boundary
  crossing counts exactly once (spec edge case). Serve heads toward the conceding
  side.
- AI_SPEED (340) deliberately < PADDLE_SPEED (460) and it recentres when the ball
  moves away → competent but beatable, never a perfect mirror.

WHY the DOM status surface:
- The canvas is opaque to a11y tools and Playwright. The engine pushes a
  PongPublicState via onStateChange ONLY when status/score change, so the island
  can render a role="status" region (data-testid hooks) without re-rendering
  React every frame. This makes E2E deterministic (assert text, not pixels) and
  improves accessibility, with no backend API/DB/persistence added.

TESTING importance:
- Pong.test.ts drives update(dt) with a stubbed 2D context to cover pure logic:
  state machine, paddle clamping, AI cap/bounds/beatability, reflection + angle
  variance, wall bounce, both-side scoring + single-count reset, win & lose at 11,
  restart, key-repeat not skipping screens, onStateChange, and destroy cleanup.
- pong.spec.ts asserts the observable browser flow against the DOM surface;
  the endgame test lets the idle player concede to 11 (~40s) to exercise real
  win/lose + restart without test-only engine hooks.

